# `A⁻¹ @ b` means the solution of A x = b: the inverse is never formed. The
# method follows the tags: LU for a general square matrix, division for a
# Diagonal one, Q⁻¹ = Qᵀ for an Orthogonal one (and Cholesky for a
# PositiveDefinite one, explained by the checker's older note). `x @ A⁻¹` is a
# transposed solve, and `A⁻¹ @ B⁻¹` is `(B @ A)⁻¹`.
A: Matrix[100, 100]
B: Matrix[100, 100]
D: Diagonal Matrix[100, 100]
Q: Orthogonal Matrix[100, 100]
P: PositiveDefinite Matrix[100, 100]
b: Vector[100]
lu = A⁻¹ @ b
right = b @ inv(A)
diag = D⁻¹ @ b
orth = Q⁻¹ @ b
chol = P⁻¹ @ b
pair = A⁻¹ @ B⁻¹
many = A⁻¹ @ B
print(lu, right, diag, orth, chol, pair, many)
